<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Kępno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1282">1282</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Kępno was an agreement made on 15 February 1282 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a>, Duke of Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Mestwin, who had no sons, named Przemysł his heir, and on Mestwin's death in 1294 Pomerelia passed to Przemysł, who was crowned King of Poland the next year. The treaty later underpinned Polish claims to Gdańsk Pomerania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kepno.jpg" alt="Treaty of Kępno" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Matejko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Przemysl_II_(76843358)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> had been divided among rival dukes since the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> in 1138. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a>, High Duke of Poland and ruler of Wielkopolska, held the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>, Wielkopolska and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, and the addition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Gdańsk Pomerania</a> to them was an important step towards reuniting the country. Under the agreement suzerainty over Gdańsk Pomerania passed to Przemysł, who took the title dux Polonie et Pomeranie, Duke of Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a> kept de facto control of the province for the rest of his life, while Przemysł became its ruler de jure. Some sources suggest that Mestwin kept full control and that the agreement only laid down that whichever of the two outlived the other would take over his lands. As custom required, an assembly of nobles of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and Wielkopolska affirmed and approved the treaty.</p><p>The first effect of the treaty was that the two dukes pursued a single foreign policy, above all towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Western Pomerania</a>. Przemysł also placed several trusted men from Wielkopolska in offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> and the surrounding area. As part of the settlement Przemysł also granted the town of Kępno municipal rights based on those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Further meetings followed at Nakło in 1284, Rzepka in 1288 and Nakło again in 1291, and another at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Słupsk</a> was attended by Bogusław IV, Duke of Szczecin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin dynasty</a>. There the three rulers concluded a mutual-defence alliance against the expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and agreed that Bogusław would inherit Pomerania if both Przemysł and Mestwin died without heirs. The alliance was reaffirmed at Nakło in 1291.</p><p>In 1296 Przemysł was assassinated by agents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a> and members of the Nałęcz family, both of whom opposed a strong central power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. His realm, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> included, then became the object of a civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a> and Władysław I the Elbow-high, each of whom at some point had himself crowned king of Poland. Although Bogusław outlived both Mestwin and Przemysł, the Nakło provisions were never carried out and he never gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Gdańsk Pomerania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Kępno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Mestwin II, who died without sons, left the duchy to Przemysl II of Greater Poland under the <b>Treaty of Kepno</b> of 1282.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerelia">Duchy of Pomerelia</a></li></ul></section>
